 had been pushed by proponents of  eugenics supporters of the law believed  doctors were refusing to sterilize women  who deserve sterilization because they  were afraid of prosecution  the new law was meant to remove that  threat  dr albert priddy saw kerry buck as a  genetic threat to society he called her  incorrigible and said that without  sterilization there was a high risk she  would produce offspring  so dr priddy filed the necessary  paperwork to sterilize kerry  his request was approved by the  institution's board of directors  but kerry's legal guardian challenged  the sterilization order in court and  that challenge eventually made its way  to the united states supreme court  carrie through her guardian argued that  involuntary sterilization would violate  her right to due process and equal  protection under the 14th amendment  the right to procreate they argued was a  fundamental right and it was a right  that could not be denied by state law  but the supreme court by an eight-to-one  boat disagreed  the court accepted that kerry was quote  feeble-minded and promiscuous and that  it was in the best interest of the state  to sterilizer  writing for the court justice oliver  wendell holmes wrote that the state's  interest in preserving the public  welfare outweighed kerry's interest in  bodily integrity and personal choice  in the words of justice holmes we have  seen more than once that the public  welfare may call upon the best citizens  for their lives it would be strange if  it could not call upon those who already  sapped the strength of the state for  these lesser sacrifices to prevent our  being swamped with incompetence  he added  three generations of imbeciles are  enough  memorable but not one of holmes's finest  moments  carrie buck received a tubal location  she was later released from the  institution  kerry by the way became an avid reader  and was so until her death in 1983  and her daughter who had been  erroneously labeled feeble-minded after  a quick examination by a eugenics field  worker turned out to be a solid student  even being listed on her school's honor  roll  the course ruling in buck versus bell  encouraged dozens of additional states  to enact compulsory sterilization laws  virginia's sterilization law remained on  the books until its repeal in 1974.  buck versus bell was not the only  supreme court case to consider mandatory  sterilization  in 1942 the court considered an oklahoma  law that required the sterilization of  all three-time felons  a sort of three-strike senior snip law  an oklahoman by the name of jack skinner  made the mistake of stealing some  chickens  it was skinner's third felony and as  part of his punishment he was ordered to  undergo a vasectomy  skinner sued and in the case of skinner  versus oklahoma the supreme court sided  with him  different justices gave different  reasons for reaching their conclusion  the majority felt the law violated  skinner's right to equal protection of  the laws  the court said the choice to procreate  was so fundamental that the state needed  a strong reason to take it away a reason  that oklahoma didn't have  chief justice stone believed the law  violated skinner's due process rights he  was entitled to a hearing on the  question of whether his criminal  tendencies were of an inheritable type  and he never got such a hearing  and for justice jackson oklahoma's law  violated both equal protection and due  process justice jackson wrote there are  limits to the extent to which a  legislatively represented majority may  conduct biological experiments at the  expense of the dignity of a minority  even those who have been guilty of what  the majority defined as crimes  after skinner and after the public  learned about the eugenics program of  nazi germany sterilization rates dropped  dramatically  by the 1960s restrictions on the use of  contraceptives became more of an issue  than sterilization laws  two cases challenging restrictions on  the distribution of contraceptives  reached the court and were decided on  their merits  the first was a case of griswold versus  connecticut  in griswold the court found a state law  banning the distribution of  contraceptives to be an unconstitutional  burden on the right of marital privacy  which justice william o douglas found to  be implicit in the bill of rights  in a wonderfully metaphysical opinion  justice douglas talked about the various  protections of the bill of rights having  penumbras and emanations that  effectively extended their reach  granted nothing in the constitution  specifically says states can't regulate  the use of contraceptives but no matter  douglas argued that the emanations of  the first third fourth and fifth  amendments made obvious the framers  intention to create a zone of privacy  that the state could not invade  and threatening married couples with  jail time for using contraceptives was  just such an unconstitutional invasion  in a concurring opinion the usually  conservative justice john harlan  conjured up the image of police officers  snooping around in what he called the  sacred precincts of the marital bedroom  for telltale evidence of contraceptive  use  in another interesting concurring  opinion justice arthur goldberg saw the  connecticut law as a violation of the  ninth amendment which reads  the enumeration in the constitution of  certain rights shall not be construed to  deny or disparage others retained by the  people  goldberg quoted james madison's reasons  for drafting the ninth amendment  madison said  it's been objected also against a bill  of rights that by enumerating particular  exceptions to the grant of power it  would disparage those rights which were  not placed in that enumeration and it  might follow by implication that those  rights which were not singled out were  intended to be assigned into the hands  of the general government and were  consequently  insecure  this is one of the most plausible  arguments i have ever heard urged  against the admission of a bill of  rights into the system but i conceive  that it may be guarded against i have  attempted it as gentlemen may see by  turning to the last clause of the ninth  amendment  over the years many judges and legal  scholars from the original school have  criticized the interpretation of  griswold versus connecticut  famously judge robert bork's criticism  of the decision and its recognition of a  privacy right proved to be his undoing  in the hearings on his nomination to the  supreme court  judge bork compared the ninth amendment  to an ink blot on the constitution  we can't figure out what madison and the  framers meant  so let's not even try  griswold recognized a right of marital  privacy a right that included the right  to possess and use contraceptives  but it left open the question of whether  the constitution protected the right of  unmarried persons to use contraceptives  some states argue that allowing single  people to use contraceptives would lead  to promiscuity  a former medical student named william  baird challenged the legal validity of  that argument  in 1965 in new york baird engaged in an  unusual form of civil disobedience he  handed out contraceptives to anyone who  wanted them  it was illegal at the time to distribute  contraceptives without a license  baird was arrested and prosecuted and as  a result of his protest he lost his job  with a pharmaceutical company  but baird remained undeterred two years  later in massachusetts he was at it  again  massachusetts law allowed only married  persons to obtain contraceptives and  then only with a doctor's prescription  so baird showed up for a speaking  engagement at boston university with  several boxes of contraceptives  about 2 000 people mostly students were  intended in attendance and during his  speech baird asked volunteers to come  forward to distribute contraceptives  contraceptive foam and condense to  anyone in the audience who wanted them  then he asked the police in the hall to  come and arrest him  his goal of course was to bring a test  case challenging the massachusetts ban  on the distribution of contraceptives to  unmarried persons  he was arrested and booked entered a cle  a plea of not guilty and he was released  on bail  well  things did not work out well in the  state courts for baird  he was convicted in a non-jury trial and  then the massachusetts supreme court on  a 4-3 vote  upheld his conviction and his  three-month prison zones  barrett spent 35 days in the charles  street jail before the u.s supreme court  agreed to hear his appeal  the case known as eisenstadt versus  baird was decided in 1972.  the majority voted to overturn baird's  conviction  writing for the court just as william  brennan inserted language that would  prove useful in another majority opinion  decided soon thereafter  roe versus wade  brennan wrote if the right of privacy  means anything it is the right of the  individual married or single to be free  from unwarranted governmental intrusion  into matters so fundamentally affecting  a person is a decision whether to bear  or beget a child  and so we come to one of the most  controversial decisions ever made by the  u.s supreme court  a case is centered on the claim of a  young woman named norma mccorvey  now norma mccorvey could do one thing  well  shoot pool  in 1969 at age 21 she liked to play at a  mostly lesbian bar called the white  carriage in dallas  mccorvey organized tournaments  held the bets and divided up the prize  money  some straight men came and liked to go  to the carriage to shoot pool with the  women  one saturday night a man who mccorvey  thought to be about 50 years old asked  her to be her pool partner  norma later gave the man the name carl  but that wasn't his real name  carl was a good pool player he and  mccorvey won their matches that evening  and took home 100 bucks  the two began to play together regularly  at bars around dallas  and eventually mccorvey started sleeping  with carl  they'd rank together drop some acid  together  mccarvey wrote later of their  relationship we were rebels outlaws  together  but she knew it wouldn't last  it ended when carl dropped norma off at  her mother's trailer in louisiana  he waved and headed out on his way to  las vegas  norma would never see carl again  in louisiana mccorvey landed a job  running an animal freak show at a  traveling carnival she stood outside a  tent each evening pitching the show and  selling tickets  with 30 dollars to her name sleeping in  a carnival trunk  norma realized she was pregnant  she described herself as numb past  caring  a pregnant redneck hippie carnie  she took a bus back to dallas  norma said she couldn't bear to think of  carrying a baby  it was simply a thing growing inside me  getting bigger every day  she didn't want the child to be born  with me as its mother  a friend told mccorvey that a doctor  might be able to terminate her pregnancy  interestingly the word abortion was  completely foreign to her  she visited her  obstetrician  the same doctor who had delivered two  previous babies said she'd given up for  adoption  the doctor told her that he did not  perform abortions  in fact if you learned of anyone doing  them he'd be obligated to report them  he told her you really should have  thought of this before you got pregnant  before she left the doctor gave her the  phone number of a lawyer  norma mccorvey met with the lawyer in  his office at first he tried to talk  mccorvey into putting the baby up for  adoption  but corby said she'd prefer to get an  abortion  wherever she can find it  you might well get yourself killed you  warned then he said  i know a couple of young lawyers who are  looking for a pregnant woman who wants  an abortion  a woman just like you  they need a plaintiff in a lawsuit to  help them overturn the texas law against  abortions  in february 1970 in an italian  restaurant in dallas mccorvey met the  two lawyers who would take her case to  the supreme court of the united states  linda coffey and sarah weddington  weddington asked mccorvey if she really  wanted an abortion and why  corby said that pregnancy was making her  life miserable  it made it almost impossible for her to  find work  mostly though she complained she didn't  want this thing as she called it growing  inside her body  weddington told mccorvey stories of  shady doctors and botched back alley  abortions then she asked norma if she  agreed that all women should have access  to safe and legal abortions  she said sure of course  and then she began to cry  weddington and coffey filed a lawsuit  against the state of texas and norman  mccarvey became the lead plaintiff jane  rowe in the case that would eventually  become known as roe vs wade  but mccorvey was already about six  months pregnant  and getting a case to the supreme court  takes years not weeks  mccarvey spent the next couple of months  smoking dope drinking wine and living  with what she called a group of hippies  she tried not to think about her  pregnancy  she wasn't even present when her lawsuit  was heard by a panel of three federal  district court judges  the panel handed her a victory of sorts  striking down the texas law that  prohibited abortions except when  necessary to save the life of the mother  but that victory proved hollow when  district attorney henry wade appealed  the case and the order was stayed  pending the appeal  wade announced that he would prosecute  any doctor who performed an abortion  mccorvey then realized what should have  been obvious to a first-year law student  this case was not really about her  she felt used and she took the news hard  norma's water broke in the middle of the  night her baby was delivered within  minutes after arrival in the emergency  room and was put up for adoption  norma felt depressed and spent the next  several days drinking heavily  she swallowed dozens of pills to end it  all  she hallucinated lost consciousness but  woke up a day later still alive  the birth of jane rose that is norma  mccorvey's baby did not moot the case  the suit was brought as a class action  on behalf not only of mccorvey but also  unnamed pregnant women in the state of  texas who might desire an abortion  in march 1971 the supreme court  announced that it would hear the case of  roe versus wade  sarah weddington moved from austin to  new york city to work with women's  rights lawyers on preparation for the  argument  the court had actually taken the road  case not to decide the abortion issue  but rather to consider another question  raised by the case specifically whether  women and doctors who wish to challenge  state abortion laws could go first to  federal court rather than going through  all the possible appeals at the state  level first  in oral arguments sarah weddington  seemed to be surprised by the court's  focus on the jurisdictional issue  and on the substantive question of what  provision in the constitution supported  a right to abortion she seemed unsure of  where to point  after weddington rattled off a long list  of potential economic and psychological  harms associated with giving birth to an  unwanted child  just as potter steward tried to steer to  the constitutional text  he noted rather paternalistically  sometimes in the court we would like to  sometimes but we cannot be here involved  simply with matters of policy as you  know  weddington took the hint  we originally brought the suit alleging  the due process clause the equal  protection clause the ninth amendment  and a variety of others she said  justice stewart interrupted and asked  sarcastically and anything else that  might be applicable  yes yeah weddington answered as many in  the courtroom laughed  justice byron white asked whether  weddington was claiming that women had a  right to choose an abortion even late in  their pregnancies  well do you or don't you say that the  constitutional right you insist on  reaches up to the time of birth  weddington gave the most radical answer  possible  suggesting that the constitution gives  protection to people only after birth  until then a woman's right to choose  should be in force  birth is not the point at which the  court could ever draw the line  an abortion at eight and a half months  just seems too close to infanticide  but the court seemed equally  uncomfortable with the line proposed by  assistant attorney general of texas jay  floyd  asked when a fetus has full  constitutional protection floyd answered  at any time there is life from the  moment of impregnation  when floyd pointed to a section of the  state's brief showing the development of  the fetus beginning at seven days after  conception justice thurgood marshall  jumped in  well what about six days we don't know  floyd replied but the statute goes all  the way back to one hour marshall  pointed out  there are unanswerable questions in this  field floyd conceded  only seven justices gathered in the  supreme court conference room to decide  the row case two vacancies remained  unfilled  the next year justice rehnquist and  powell would join the court  to the surprise of the three most  liberal justices on the court the  jurisdictional issue was suddenly off  the table  swing justice potter stewart joined them  in another case decided the same day  allowing a bookstore owner to challenge  a state obscenity law in federal court  before exhausting state court remedies  the same principles would have to apply  in row  so the court would reach the merits  after all and on the merits there were  three solid votes just write down the  texas law  justices marshall brennan and douglas  two justices favored a narrow ruling  against portions of the law  justices blackmon and steward seemed  ready to base their decision on the  right of doctors not women a right to  exercise their professional judgment  free of state interference  chief justice warren berger assigned the  task of writing the court's opinion to  justice harry blackman  at this early point in his supreme court  career had a mostly conservative voting  record  but before joining the court he had  served as the general counsel to the  mayo clinic  he had a great respect for doctors and  saw the doctor-patient relationship as  something close to sacred  blackman loved working with the surgical  staff he called the 10 years at mayo the  best of his life  one of his jobs had been to advise the  staff on the legality of abortions the  hospital performed  and many of those he approved would not  have been legal in the state of texas  blackman poured hundreds of hours into  crafting an opinion he worked through  the fall and winter doing research in  the court's library he read both medical  and legal texts taking careful notes  he discovered that abortion had  generally been legal in the united  states until the 19th century the bans  were put in place to protect pregnant  women against what was at the time a  dangerous operation  but now abortions were safer than  childbirth to blackman that fact argued  strongly in favor of legalizing early  abortions  in may of 1972 blackman finally  circulated his draft opinion to the  other justices but the opinion was short  on analysis  it indicated that women had a right to  privacy that included a right to choose  an abortion but it didn't say where in  the constitution that right came from  the opinion simply concluded the texas  law was too vague to be constitutional  black men seemed to suggest that  abortion should be legal until the time  the fetus became viable outside of the  womb  but it didn't directly say that laws  against early abortions were always  unconstitutional  hardly the sort of guidance the medical  profession would like  blackman became convinced of his opinion  shortcomings especially after reading  dissent from justice white  he withdrew his opinion  meanwhile justices renquist and powell  took their seats on the court  lewis powell's father-in-law had been a  prominent obstetrician  confident that he could get powell's  vote blackman urged roe to be re-argued  the next term  and over the strong objection of justice  william o douglas the court voted to do  just that  blackman spent much of the summer of  1972 in minnesota researching abortion  at the mayo clinics library he reworked  his draft to make the right of privacy  explicit  women had a constitutional right to  choose whether or not to bear a child  here the contraceptive cases present  provided the precedent  the court said in eisenstadt women  should be able to choose whether or not  to bear a child  the right to an abortion he wrote was  not absolute the state had a compelling  interest in regulating abortions to  protect women's health especially after  three months when the risks increase  and at some point the interest of the  state in protecting the life of the  fetus overrode the privacy interests  for lack of a better place to draw the  line blackman said that the point came  about six months into the pregnancy  so rose trimester framework emerged  blackman also declared that a fetus was  not a person in the constitutional sense  the right to liberty under the  constitution attaches only after birth  of course if fetuses were persons within  the meaning of the constitution  abortions would be a gross violation of  rights  the case was re-argued in october but it  was nearly a pointless exercise  the votes were in six justices joined  blackman in a 7-2 decision issued on  january 22 1973.  in his dissent justice white offered  strong words  i find nothing in the language or  history of the constitution to support  the court's judgment as an exercise of  raw judicial power the court perhaps has  authority to do what it does today but  in my view its judgment is an  improvement and extravagant exercise of  the power of judicial review  the public reaction was even stronger  cardinal terence cook asked how many  millions of children will never live to  see the light of day because of this  shocking decision of the supreme court  today  cardinal john kroll said it was hard to  think of any decision in the 200 years  of our history that had more disastrous  implications for his stability as a  civilized society  the state of texas filed a petition for  re-hearing comparing the decision to  dred scott  norma mccorvey found out she won her  case at the kitchen table in a house she  shared with connie gonzalez the woman  who became her lover and partner  she read the story on the front page of  the dallas times herald  norma had never told connie about her  role in the abortion case  she asked connie  how would you like to meet jane rowe  but mccorvey's views would change she  converted to catholicism by 1997 she was  ardently pro-life and even participated  in anti-abortion protests  norma died in 2017 at the age of 69.  roe vs wade was more the beginning of  the abortion debate than the end of it  the core liberty protected by roe  remains at least at the time of this  lecture  but the court has abandoned roe's  trimester framework it now upholds state  regulations which do not quote  pose an undue burden on that right  roe versus wade has changed americans  politics in ways too numerous to mention  it's become a litmus test for candidates  in both major parties  it is a highly emotional and complicated  issue  abortions are at least morally dubious  and one can ask whether societal  acceptance of morally dubious actions  makes it more likely we will come to  accept morally wrong decisions  infanticide perhaps  on the other hand many americans might  understand that pregnancies and  childbirth seriously affect the lives of  women  and they might believe that when a woman  and her doctor decide abortion is the  better option little is to be gained by  locking the doctor in jail  ultimately as they say that's where the  rubber meets the road  anyone who says the abortion question is  an easy one isn't thinking very hard  about the issue  it's tough  damn tough  you

